---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '@/components/Callout.astro';
import DocsShell from '@/components/DocsShell.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security boundaries"
  description="Caurium public security boundaries: separate deployments, management access, credentials, logging and how to report a concern."
  path="/docs/security/"
  ogImage="/og/docs.png"
  ogAlt="Caurium security boundaries documentation."
>
  <DocsShell
    current="/docs/security/"
    title="Security boundaries"
    description="What is separated, what is not public, and what Caurium does not claim. This page describes the public site and the intended boundaries of planned products."
    toc={[
      { id: 'scope', label: 'Scope' },
      { id: 'website', label: 'Public website' },
      { id: 'management', label: 'Management access' },
      { id: 'credentials', label: 'Product credentials' },
      { id: 'probe', label: 'Probe and SSRF' },
      { id: 'status-data', label: 'Public status data' },
      { id: 'threat-model', label: 'Threat model' },
      { id: 'isolation', label: 'Isolation claims' },
      { id: 'reporting', label: 'Reporting gap' },
      { id: 'not-claimed', label: 'Not claimed' },
    ]}
  >
    <h2 id="scope">Scope of this page</h2>
    <p>
      This page covers the public website, the deployed Probe service and the intended security
      boundaries of Gate, Guard and Box. Probe is in <strong>private preview</strong>; Gate and Guard
      are <strong>in development</strong> and not open. Box is <strong>Planned</strong> and not
      deployed. No endpoint, console or credential is offered for any of them. Nothing here is a
      certification, an audit result or a compliance attestation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="website">Public website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The site is a static export served from Cloudflare Workers Static Assets. It has no application
        server, database or login.
      </li>
      <li>
        The site has one application form that posts to the Probe service. It has no account creation,
        payment processing or API key generation.
      </li>
      <li>The site does not set first-party cookies for analytics, advertising or personalization.</li>
      <li>
        The hosting provider processes technical request logs under its own configuration. The privacy
        page describes this without claiming that no data is processed.
      </li>
      <li>Builds do not require product, management or separately operated network credentials.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="management">Management access</h2>
    <p>
      A future management panel is a separate application with its own deployment, session and
      permissions. It is not mounted under a hidden path of the public site and is not protected by an
      unguessable URL alone.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Administrators use multi-factor authentication and do not share accounts.</li>
      <li>Owner, operator and read-only permissions are separated.</li>
      <li>Website publishing credentials cannot operate the management panel.</li>
      <li>Management cookies are host-only and are not scoped to the whole apex domain.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="credentials">Product credentials and limits (planned)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Caller credentials are issued per identity, shown once, and stored only as irreversible
        verification values.
      </li>
      <li>Upstream secrets stay in server-side secret storage and are never sent to a browser.</li>
      <li>
        Gate forwards only to an allow-listed upstream. Caller-supplied destination URLs are rejected
        so the service cannot become an open proxy.
      </li>
      <li>
        Limits cover requests, body size, timeout and spend, backed by durable storage rather than a
        single process.
      </li>
      <li>
        Logs contain request ID, endpoint identifier, status, latency and necessary usage. Prompt,
        request and response bodies are not logged by default.
      </li>
      <li>
        Automatic retries are limited to requests known to be safe; a request that may create duplicate
        cost is not retried without an idempotency guarantee.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="probe">Probe targets and SSRF</h2>
    <p>
      Probe is intended for endpoints the operator owns or is authorized to monitor. A fixed target
      list replaces anonymous URL submission. DNS resolution, redirects and the connect boundary must
      block localhost, private ranges, link-local addresses, cloud metadata endpoints and their IPv6
      equivalents. Response size and total duration are bounded.
    </p>

    <h2 id="status-data">Public status data</h2>
    <p>
      Public status contains a component alias, coarse region, health state and timestamps. It excludes
      origin addresses, internal hostnames, full check URLs and raw error stacks. The publisher has no
      scanning ability and no management credentials.
    </p>

    <h2 id="threat-model">Threat model</h2>
    <p>
      The table records the asset, the adversary considered and the intended control. A status of
      Current means the control applies to this public site. Planned means it is part of a module’s
      design and has not been verified in a deployment.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Asset</th>
            <th scope="col">Adversary</th>
            <th scope="col">Control</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Public site build</th>
            <td>Someone who can push a commit</td>
            <td>
              No product, management or network secrets in the build; repository permissions separate
              from operations.
            </td>
            <td>Current</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Caller credential</th>
            <td>A leaked or shared key</td>
            <td>
              Per-caller keys, irreversible verification storage, independent revocation and limits.
            </td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Upstream secret</th>
            <td>A compromised product service</td>
            <td>
              Server-side secret storage; never sent to a browser; not present in the public build.
            </td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Management session</th>
            <td>Credential theft or a hidden-path guess</td>
            <td>Separate application, own identity, MFA, host-only cookies, no shared accounts.</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Probe target</th>
            <td>An attacker using the checker to reach internal networks</td>
            <td>
              Fixed target list, rejects localhost, private IPv4 literals, metadata hosts and unsafe
              redirect hops; bounded timeout; response bodies discarded.
            </td>
            <td>Current</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Status publisher</th>
            <td>Compromise of the public status path</td>
            <td>
              Sanitized schema only; no origin URLs or raw errors. Collection and publication still
              share one Worker and must be split before a stronger boundary is claimed.
            </td>
            <td>Current, with a known gap</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Backup copy</th>
            <td>Theft of stored backup data</td>
            <td>Encryption before transfer; recovery keys stored separately from the copies.</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="isolation">Isolation claims</h2>
    <p>
      Box environments are container-based and share a kernel. They are not described as microVMs,
      virtual machines or VM-level isolation. No module claims a security property that has not been
      tested and recorded.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reporting">Reporting gap</h2>
    <Callout title="No public security contact is published yet." tone="warning">
      A reporting channel and response expectations will be published before paid activation opens.
      Until then, do not send credentials, tokens, private addresses or other sensitive material
      through any public channel. This gap is recorded as a production blocker in the content audit.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="not-claimed">What is not claimed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA or other certification.</li>
      <li>No penetration-test result or bug-bounty program.</li>
      <li>No availability guarantee, SLA or 24/7 response commitment.</li>
      <li>
        No claim that publishing this site reduces the chance of interference with separately operated
        services.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For the stage definitions, read <a href="/docs/product-stages/">Product stages</a>. For the
      site’s actual data handling, read <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
    </p>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
